    "faq": {
      "type": "dvp-faq",
      "settings": {
        "anchor_id": "faq",
        "eyebrow": "Questions homeowners ask",
        "heading": "Deck vs patio, Faqs",
        "intro": "Still weighing it up? A deck specialist can look at your yard with you.",
        "button_label": "Talk to a Deck Specialist",
        "button_link": "#chat",
        "open_first": true,
        "faq_schema": true
      },
      "blocks": {
        "question_1": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What is the difference between a deck and a patio?",
            "answer": "<p>A deck is a raised platform of wood or composite boards on a frame, supported by footings. A patio is a paved surface, such as concrete, pavers or stone, laid directly on prepared ground.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_2": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is a deck considered a patio?",
            "answer": "<p>No. A deck is a raised platform of wood or composite boards on a frame and footings. A patio is a hard surface, such as pavers, concrete or stone, laid on the ground. A ground-level deck can sit as low as a patio, but it’s still a deck.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_3": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Which lasts longer, a deck or a patio?",
            "answer": "<p>A paver patio can last 30–50 years and concrete 25–50, as long as the base holds. A wood deck typically lasts 10–20+ years with regular resealing, and composite decking 25–30 years. The bigger difference is upkeep: a patio’s problems start underground, while a deck’s wear stays on the surface where it’s easy to fix.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_4": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is a deck or a patio easier to maintain?",
            "answer": "<p>A composite deck is the easiest: wash it once or twice a year. A wood deck needs cleaning and resealing every 2–3 years. A paver patio needs weeding and re-sanding, and sunken areas mean lifting stones and rebuilding the base.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_5": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Deck or patio: which adds more resale value to a home?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes, a deck adds value to a home, and decks have the clearer data. In the 2025 Cost vs Value report, a wood deck addition recouped 94.9% of its cost at resale nationally, and a composite deck recouped 88.5%. Patios aren’t included in that report, so there’s no comparable national figure.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_6": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Deck vs patio vs porch vs balcony: what’s the difference?",
            "answer": "<p>A deck is an open platform on footings, usually off the back of the house. A porch is a covered space attached to the house, typically at an entrance. A balcony is an elevated platform on an upper floor that you reach from inside. In most backyards, the real choice is between a deck and a patio.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_7": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Deck vs pavers: which works better on a sloped yard?",
            "answer": "<p>A deck. Its footings set the height, so the floor stays level while the ground slopes underneath. Pavers need a flat, compacted base, so a paver patio on a slope usually means regrading or retaining walls.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_8": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Concrete patio vs wood or composite deck: which is better?",
            "answer": "<p>It depends on your ground. A concrete patio needs a flat, well-prepared base and is permanent once poured. A wood deck stands on footings, handles slopes and damp soil, and feels softer underfoot. Wood needs cleaning and resealing now and then; composite just needs cleaning.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_9": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Raised patio vs deck: which is easier to build?",
            "answer": "<p>Usually the deck. A raised patio needs retaining walls and compacted fill to hold the surface up. A deck holds itself up on footings and a frame, and an A-DIY kit goes together with a drill, with no cutting or measuring.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_10": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Floating deck vs patio: is a ground-level deck better?",
            "answer": "<p>It depends on what you want underfoot. A floating (ground-level) deck and a patio both sit low to the yard, but the deck adds a finished, drained floor that feels softer than stone and needs no excavation. A patio gives you a hard surface for a grill or fire pit.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_11": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can you build a deck over a concrete patio?",
            "answer": "<p>Often, yes. A low deck can sit over a sound, well-drained slab. Check that the concrete isn’t cracked or heaving, that water can still run off, and that your local code allows it.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_12": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I build a deck myself without special tools?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. A-DIY kits are engineered so there’s no cutting or measuring. A power drill is the only tool you need, and the custom drill bit comes in the box.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_13": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do I need a permit to build a deck?",
            "answer": "<p>It depends on your town. Some codes exempt low, freestanding decks under a set height and size; others don’t. Check with your local building department before you build.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_14": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What comes in an A-DIY deck kit?",
            "answer": "<p>Engineered framing (beams and joists), deck boards, galvanized screws and hardware, Tuff Block footings, a custom drill bit and a step-by-step assembly guide.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
